---
/**
 * Intro   Dolní Morava, Stezka v oblacích, Sky Bridge 721.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY THIS IS NOT A `.split` ANY MORE
 *
 * It was: photograph in the right half, copy in the left, the primitive every
 * other section on the site is built from. On `/okoli` that is the wrong shape
 * for two reasons.
 *
 * The first is repetition. This page is the one that has to hold somebody's
 * attention through five sections of lists, and it opened on the same split the
 * homepage opens on, with the SAME PHOTOGRAPH   dolnimorava.webp is the only
 * landscape in the asset set and DolniMorava.astro already uses it. Two splits
 * of one picture is not an opener, it is a page repeating itself.
 *
 * The second is the drift. `[data-drift]` existed in exactly one place on this
 * site (DolniMorava.astro) and moved exactly one photograph inside one half of
 * one split, where there is nothing beside it standing still to measure it
 * against. An effect with nothing to compare itself to is not depth; it is a
 * picture that will not sit down.
 *
 * ---- SO: A STAGE WITH THREE LAYERS IN IT ----
 * Back to front, each moving at its own rate, which is the only thing that makes
 * a parallax read as one:
 *
 *   1. THE PHOTOGRAPH   `--drift-shift: 6%`, downward. Oversized 1.18 so it has
 *      somewhere to go, and clipped by `.intro` rather than by `.photo-frame`,
 *      which only clips under `@media (hover: hover)` and would let the picture
 *      slide out across the section boundary on a phone.
 *
 *   2. THE WOOD   Treeline.astro, the footer's own drawing, laid along the
 *      bottom edge at `--drift-shift: 2.4%`. It travels a bit over a third as
 *      far as the photograph, so the trees separate from the mountain behind
 *      them as the section crosses the viewport. This is the layer that does the
 *      work: two layers is a moving picture, three is a place with depth in it.
 *      It is also honest about the geography   the resort is something seen from
 *      inside the wood the cottage stands in.
 *
 *   3. THE COPY PANEL   `--drift-shift: -1.6%`, and note the MINUS. It rises
 *      while the other two fall, which is what puts the reader's eye in front of
 *      the scene rather than on it, and it is the reason this section counts as
 *      "content in the parallax" rather than "a photograph that moves".
 *      `--drift-scale` stays at its default 1: a scaled text layer is a blurred
 *      text layer.
 *
 * All three stop dead under `prefers-reduced-motion` and all three are simply
 * still where `animation-timeline: view()` does not exist   the primitive in
 * global.css carries that contract, and the section is composed so that a
 * completely static render is a perfectly good static render.
 *
 * ⚠ THE PANEL IS OPAQUE --omitka, NOT TYPE ON A SCRIM.
 * PageHero directly above this sets --omitka type straight onto a darkened
 * photograph. Doing the same thing here would make the page open on two
 * scrimmed photo bands in a row, and it would put four paragraphs and a pair of
 * figures over a picture whose luminance runs from black spruce to lit snow in
 * the space of 200 px. An opaque panel means the copy keeps the page's ordinary
 * contrast pairings (--les on --omitka, 15.45:1) and the photograph keeps its
 * highlights.
 *
 * CONTENT is unchanged from the version this replaces   docs/01-content.md §4.8
 * and §7.1: `bobová dráha` not `bobový` (error #9), one spelling of
 * `Sky Bridge 721`, `Červená Voda` capitalised as the place name it is, and the
 * §4.8 original's verb-less subordinate clause rewritten into whole sentences.
 *
 * We do NOT name the Sněžník summit trail distance here. The old copy says 14 km,
 * the regional map says 17 km, the data holds null (docs/01-content.md §9 item 18).
 * Naming either next to a map that contradicts it is worse than naming neither.
 *
 * Band: the section ground is --les, seen only at the extremes of the drift and
 * behind the wood. The panel is --omitka. The map section below is --kamen.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import sjezdovky from '@img/dolnimorava.webp';
import EdgePines from '../../decor/EdgePines.astro';

/* U+00A0   non-breaking space. Binds numerals to their units and single-letter
   prepositions to the word that follows (docs/02-design-system.md §2.6). */
const NBSP = ' ';
---

<section class="intro" aria-labelledby="intro-nadpis">
  <div class="intro-scena" aria-hidden="true">
    <Image
      src={sjezdovky}
      alt=""
      widths={[560, 900, 1200, 1600]}
      sizes="100vw"
      width={950}
      height={789}
      format="webp"
      quality={64}
      loading="lazy"
      class="intro-foto"
      data-drift
      style="--drift-scale: 1.18; --drift-shift: 6%"
    />

    {/*
      The photograph is decorative here   `alt=""` above   because the copy panel
      in front of it names the resort, the pistes, the walkway and the bridge in
      full. A screen reader that has just been read four sentences about Dolní
      Morava does not need "letecký pohled na zasněžený areál Dolní Morava"
      immediately afterwards. The same photograph carries its full alt on the
      homepage, where nothing else describes it.
    */}

    <div class="intro-scrim"></div>

    <div class="intro-les" data-drift style="--drift-shift: 4%">
      <EdgePines class="intro-les-svg" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap intro-wrap">
    <div class="intro-panel" data-drift style="--drift-shift: -1.6%" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="intro-nadpis">
        Dolní Morava, Stezka{NBSP}v oblacích, Sky Bridge{NBSP}721
      </h2>
      <p class="intro-lead">
        Hlavní turistická atrakce v okolí je ski areál Dolní Morava – vzdušnou čarou
        asi 3{NBSP}km, autem 12{NBSP}minut.
      </p>
      <p>
        Resort nabízí více než deset kilometrů sjezdovek s umělým zasněžováním,
        v zimě pro lyžování a snowboarding, v létě na sjezdy kol. Nad hřebenem stojí
        Stezka v oblacích a od ní vede visutý most Sky Bridge{NBSP}721 – nejdelší visutý
        most pro pěší na světě.
      </p>
      <p>
        Celoročně je v provozu bobová dráha a dětské zábavní areály: Mamutíkův vodní
        park, Lesní zážitkový park a Pískový svět. V okolí jsou i sjezdovky v Červené
        Vodě a Čenkovicích a turistické stezky, na které se dá napojit přímo u chalupy.
      </p>

      {/*
        Decorative: both figures are in the sentences above and in the trip index
        further down the page. Set as a pair of numbers under one rule, the way a
        printed guide sets a fact box   the same shape DolniMorava.astro uses, and
        deliberately the same, because it is the site's way of setting two figures.
      */}
      <dl class="intro-fakta" aria-hidden="true">
        <div>
          <dt>12{NBSP}min</dt>
          <dd>autem z chalupy</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>10{NBSP}km</dt>
          <dd>sjezdovek</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /*
   * The clip is unconditional and load-bearing: every layer inside the stage is
   * oversized or moving, and `.photo-frame` only clips inside
   * `@media (hover: hover)`, which on a phone would let the photograph slide out
   * across the section boundary.
   *
   * ⚠ `clip`, NEVER `hidden`. `overflow: hidden` creates a scroll container, and
   * `view()` resolves against the nearest ancestor scroll container   so all
   * three layers below would take their timeline from THIS box, which never
   * scrolls, and would sit frozen at 0.500 progress: correctly framed, correctly
   * scaled, and completely static. That is not a hypothetical; it is what the
   * homepage's Dolní Morava section did from the day it was written until this
   * primitive was extracted. The full write-up is over `[data-drift]` in
   * global.css.
   */
  .intro {
    position: relative;
    overflow: clip;
    background-color: var(--color-les);
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* ---- The stage --------------------------------------------------------
   *
   * On a phone it is an in-flow letterbox with the panel pulled up over its
   * bottom edge; from 64rem it goes out of flow and fills the whole section, so
   * the photograph runs the full width of the page behind a panel that occupies
   * a little under half of it. Two arrangements, one DOM.
   */
  .intro-scena {
    position: relative;
    height: 17rem;
    z-index: 0;
  }

  @media (width >= 64rem) {
    .intro-scena {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      height: auto;
    }
  }

  /*
   * Out of flow so it covers whatever height the stage turns out to be  
   * `height: 100%` on an in-flow image resolves against a parent with no
   * definite height and falls back to the intrinsic ratio.
   */
  .intro-foto {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 42%;
  }

  /*
   * A light --les wash, not a scrim in the PageHero sense   no text sits on the
   * photograph here. It is there to stop a bright snowfield from out-shouting an
   * --omitka panel laid on top of it, and to give the --les treeline something
   * fractionally darker than lit snow to be a silhouette against.
   */
  .intro-scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      rgb(22 36 28 / 0.28) 0%,
      rgb(22 36 28 / 0.12) 45%,
      rgb(22 36 28 / 0.45) 100%
    );
  }

  /* ---- The wood ---------------------------------------------------------
   *
   * EdgePines.astro   the same pair of spruce DolniMorava.astro stands at the
   * left of its photograph, here MIRRORED to the right-hand edge.
   *
   * ⚠ NOT Treeline.astro, and the reason is geometry rather than taste. That
   * drawing is 1440 �  332 set `xMidYMax slice`, so it crops the SIDES only while
   * its box stays at or above height/width = 0.2306   below that the crop moves
   * to the TOP and shears the tips off the tallest trees, which stand at the left
   * and right edges and therefore make it read as "the sides are cut off". A band
   * of wood at a believable middle distance behind this panel wants to be about
   * 200 px tall on a laptop, and 200/1440 is 0.14. There is no `--treeline-h`
   * that is both short enough for this stage and legal for that drawing.
   * EdgePines is `xMinYMax meet`, scales down to whatever box it is given and is
   * never clipped, so it takes a height in per cent without any of that.
   *
   * The mirror is on the SVG and the drift is on the wrapper. They must not swap
   * places or share an element: both are `transform`, and the second one to be
   * declared would simply replace the first.
   *
   * `bottom` is negative by more than the drift's travel, so across the whole
   * range the feet stay below the section's lower edge. Let them rise above it
   * and the trees are standing on a line of photograph with nothing under them.
   */
  .intro-les {
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    bottom: -5%;
    height: 62%;
  }

  .intro-les-svg {
    height: 100%;
    width: auto;
    /* Mirrored: EdgePines is drawn with its cut flank on the LEFT, and this copy
       is flush with the right-hand edge of the frame. */
    transform: scaleX(-1);
  }

  @media (width < 64rem) {
    .intro-les {
      /* A wide letterbox rather than a tall stage: at 62% the pair comes out
         small enough to read as an ornament parked in a corner. */
      height: 74%;
    }
  }

  /* ---- The copy panel ---------------------------------------------------- */

  .intro-wrap {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    /* Pulls the panel up over the bottom of the letterbox on a phone. */
    margin-top: -3.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 3.5rem;
  }

  @media (width >= 64rem) {
    .intro-wrap {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      min-height: 44rem;
      margin-top: 0;
      padding-block: 6rem;
    }
  }

  .intro-panel {
    /* Opaque, so the copy keeps --les on --omitka at 15.45:1 whatever the
       photograph is doing underneath it. */
    background-color: var(--color-omitka);
    /* Square corners. A photograph in a magazine has square corners and so does
       the panel laid on it   docs/02-design-system.md §6. */
    border-top: 3px solid var(--color-mosaz);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-float);
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem 2.25rem;
  }

  @media (width >= 48rem) {
    .intro-panel {
      padding: 2.75rem 2.5rem 3rem;
    }
  }

  @media (width >= 64rem) {
    .intro-panel {
      /*
       * A little under half the 1280 container, left of centre with the right
       * side open   §3, and here the open side is the mountain.
       */
      max-width: 38rem;
      padding: 3rem 3rem 3.25rem;
    }
  }

  h2 {
    /* --mosaz-tmava on --omitka = 6.05:1 */
    color: var(--color-mosaz-tmava);
  }

  .intro-lead {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    font-size: var(--text-lead);
    /* --les on --omitka = 15.45:1 */
    color: var(--color-les);
  }

  p {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    /* --kura on --omitka = 11.92:1 */
    color: var(--color-kura);
  }

  /* ---- The two figures --------------------------------------------------
   * One hairline over both, the pair on a two-column grid so each caption sits
   * under its own figure. The rule is the only chrome   no fill, no border box,
   * nothing that turns two numbers into two badges.
   */
  .intro-fakta {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
    max-width: 22rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgb(140 99 24 / 0.35);
  }

  .intro-fakta dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-title-s);
    line-height: 1.1;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--color-mosaz-tmava);
  }

  .intro-fakta dd {
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-caption);
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--color-kura);
  }
</style>
